<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volhynia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Volhynia is a historical region in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> until 1569. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a>, Polish negotiators secured the transfer of Volhynia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volhynia.jpg" alt="Volhynia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Сергій Венцеславський · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%27%D1%97%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0_(%D0%9D%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%B0)_%D0%B1%D0%B0%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%9B%D1%83%D1%86%D1%8C%D0%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Volhynia is a historic region lying between south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Its borders are not clearly defined. In Ukraine it corresponds roughly to Volyn Oblast, Rivne Oblast, the western part of Zhytomyr Oblast and the northern parts of Khmelnytskyi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ternopil">Ternopil</a> oblasts. Geographically it occupies the northern areas of the Volhynian-Podolian Upland and the western part of the Polesian Lowland along the Pripyat valley, in the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Western Bug</a> and the Pripyat. Its alternative name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, comes from the city of Volodymyr, once the capital of the medieval Volhynian Principality. Important cities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a>, Rivne, Kovel and Volodymyr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> names the tribes of the Dulebes, Buzhans and Volhynians. Volhynia may have belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> as early as the 10th century. In 983 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> appointed his son Vsevolod as ruler of the Volhynian principality, and in 988 he founded the city of Volodymer. From the late 12th century Volhynia was closely tied to the neighbouring Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and the two were united on several occasions under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman the Great</a> from 1199, though they broke apart.</p><p>The Hungarian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a> took the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a>, which Hungarian kings used up to 1918. After the Galicia-Volhynia state broke up about 1340, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> took western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> eastern Volhynia. After 1569 Volhynia was a voivodeship within the Lesser Poland Province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795 most of Volhynia became the Volhynian Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, which forcibly liquidated the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church and transferred its buildings to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>. 7 percent Germans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a> of 1921 divided Volhynia between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> most of the region's Jewish population of about 460,000 was shot in the Holocaust, mainly between August and October 1942, and in 1943 up to 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in Volhynia were massacred by the Ukrainian Insurgent Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> annexed Volhynia to Soviet Ukraine after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and most of the remaining ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945. Ethnic Germans were also expelled. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in the 1990s Volhynia has been an integral part of Ukraine. The territory that still carries the name is Volyn Oblast. The Peresopnytsia Gospel is counted among its important relics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volhynia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Large raids, often launched in winter when rivers were frozen, followed steppe routes such as the Muravsky Trail into Muscovy or crossed into Podolia and <b>Volhynia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lithuanian tribes in the forests between the Neman and the Neris remained pagan long after their neighbours converted, organised under dukes whose number is suggested by a treaty of 1219 with Galicia–<b>Volhynia</b> that names twenty-one of them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…coveted prize, but it was contested by several branches of the dynasty and lost its primacy as other principalities grew stronger: Chernigov, Smolensk, Galicia and <b>Volhynia</b> in the south and west, Vladimir-Suzdal in the north-east, and the Novgorod Republic, which from 1136 chose and dismissed its own princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>This gave the Polish nobility the leverage to insist on terms favourable to the Crown, including the transfer of Podlachia, <b>Volhynia</b> and the large Ukrainian palatinates of Kiev and Bracław from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet partisans under Tymofiy Strokach and nationalist guerrillas of the Ukrainian Insurgent Army (UPA) both fought the occupiers; in July 1943 UPA forces under Dmytro Klyachkivsky carried out ethnic cleansing of Poles in <b>Volhynia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Decembrists were liberal military and political dissidents, many of them nobles and guard officers; the Southern Society was dominated by army officers and landowners from Left-bank and Southern Ukraine, Sloboda Ukraine and <b>Volhynia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · People</a></li></ul></section>
